WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:05.060
This is the ocean’s ‘twilight zone’.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:10.760
More than 30 metres below the surface, 
coral reefs still flourish in the gloom.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.250
But these deep reefs could be under threat

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.920
from a powerful force:

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.440
Hurricanes.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.480
Scientists have long known that shallow reefs
are vulnerable to hurricanes

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:27.940
with corals torn apart as the winds stir up the sea.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:33.140
But the reefs of the twilight zone have been
assumed&nbsp;to be protected from the worst effects.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.940
These deeper reefs have also been thought&nbsp;to&nbsp;
provide a temporary refuge

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.960
for shallow-reef species,&nbsp;which&nbsp;could
shelter in the&nbsp;deeper water.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:47.960
But new research is telling a different story.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.060
Scientists in Puerto Rico have been studying reefs

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:57.360
both before and after Hurricane Maria,
a particularly violent storm.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:03.680
They’ve already shown that the hurricane caused 
currents and upwellings deep into the twilight zone.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:09.600
Next they plan to assess damage to the deeper
corals directly in Maria’s path.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.759
The deep-reaching effects of strong hurricanes

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:18.020
would be&nbsp;bad news for species&nbsp;on the hunt
for somewhere to&nbsp;weather the storm.

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:22.540
Of course, coral reefs have been 
surviving hurricanes for millennia

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:27.680
but extreme weather events are getting
more common as the climate changes.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.920
Human activity is also causing ocean 
acidification and warming.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.420
This can lead to diseases and coral bleaching

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.320
and recent research suggests
that deeper reefs

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.260
could be just as vulnerable&nbsp;as shallow ones.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.720
Coral reefs&nbsp;provide coastlines
with a line of defence against storms.

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:51.680
So&nbsp;losing this delicate resource

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.720
could&nbsp;add yet more pressure

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.800
to regions already devastated
by the effects of climate change.

